    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Bon</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-kailash.jpg" alt="Mount Kailash" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Mount Kailash <span class="kid-tale-cr">Ondřej Žváček, CC BY 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>Bon is the old religion that began in Tibet, and its followers are called Bonpos.</p><p>Bonpos believe it was taught by a Buddha named Tonpa Shenrab and came to Tibet from lands around Mount Kailash.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tibetans.jpg" alt="Tibetans" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Tibetans <span class="kid-tale-cr">Arian Zwegers from Brussels, Belgium, CC BY 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Like Buddhists, Bonpos believe in rebirth, being born again after death.</p><p>Bon has monks who study holy books and learn to debate.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen.jpg" alt="Trisong Detsen" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Trisong Detsen <span class="kid-tale-cr">Unknown author, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Bon histories say the Buddhist king Trisong Detsen treated Bon harshly, and its books were hidden as treasures.</p><p>Scholars say Bon took its present form more than 900 years ago, when hidden books were brought out again.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama.jpg" alt="5th Dalai Lama" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>5th Dalai Lama <span class="kid-tale-cr">Unknown author, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Fifth Dalai Lama took some Bon monasteries by force, yet he recognised Bon as a Tibetan religion.</p><p>Bonpos suffered during an invasion and later in the Cultural Revolution, but their monasteries were allowed to rebuild.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Today about 400,000 Bonpos live on the Tibetan Plateau, the high land of Tibet.</p><p>Others follow Bon in the Himalayas, in India and Nepal, and in cities around the world.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Bonpos hold that Bon is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>’s original religion, taught by the Buddha Tonpa Shenrab and brought to Tibet through Zhangzhung; many Western scholars see it as a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> that keeps genuinely ancient pre-Buddhist elements.</li><li>Its teachings closely resemble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, especially the Nyingma school, with karma, rebirth, monasticism and Dzogchen, but its authority comes from Zhangzhung rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and it keeps rituals for divination, the dead and local gods.</li><li>Long cast as an adversary in Buddhist histories, Bon suffered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> invasion of 1717 and in the Cultural Revolution, and was recognised as a Tibetan religion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a> and again, in exile, in 1979.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In Bon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> is worshipped as a holy king and master of magic, divination and astrology, and is seen as a reincarnation of Tonpa Shenrab.</p></aside>
    </section>

