    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 100 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">According to Robert Thurman, King Pudegungyal of the Yarlung dynasty institutes a Bon court religion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">8th century</span><span class="rf-text">Bon declines under the Buddhist king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> (r. 740–797); Bon histories say its texts were hidden as treasures</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">11th century</span><span class="rf-text">Treasure revealers such as Shenchen Luga (996–1035) bring out Bon texts as Yungdrung Bon takes shape</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">14th century</span><span class="rf-text">Loden Nyingpo (1360–1385) reveals the Zibji, the life of Tonpa Shenrab, and New Bon begins in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1405</span><span class="rf-text">Menri monastery, the most important Bon monastery, is built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1717</span><span class="rf-text">Many Bonpos and Nyingmapas are executed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1950</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, where there are about 300 Bon monasteries; Bonpo scholars begin to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1979</span><span class="rf-text">The Central Tibetan Administration in exile accepts Bon as a religious group with the same rights as the Buddhist schools</span></li></ol>
    </section>

